# Source: HoareLogic.pdf, section "Arrays", "A first attempt: treating a cell as a variable", "Aliasing"
# and "Back to the counterexample".
id: naive-write-rule
title: Why the naive array rule is wrong
topic: Arrays
level: 3
source: HoareLogic.pdf, Arrays, "Aliasing" and "Back to the counterexample"
tags: arrays, aliasing, soundness, counterexample

task:
  A first idea for writing into an array is to treat the cell $a[I]$ as a
  variable. This naive rule gives the triple
  $\{Q[a[I] <- E] && def(a[I])\}$ `a[I] := E` $\{Q\}$. Find values that
  break the triple it gives for `a[i] := 1` with the postcondition
  $a[j] = 0$. Then calculate the weakest precondition with ARRAY-ASSIGN,
  and use the consequence rule to decide whether the triple below is
  valid.

pre: j != i && a[j] = 0 && 0 <= i < sizeof(a)
post: a[j] = 0

program:
  {P}
  {R1}
  a[i] := 1     // i1
  {Q}

verdict: valid

step: The triple given by the naive rule
rule: LOGIC
focus: i1
text:
  The naive rule replaces the text $a[i]$ in $Q$ by $1$. $Q$ is written
  with $a[j]$, so there is nothing to replace, and the rule gives:
vc: {a[j] = 0 && def(a[i])} a[i] := 1 {a[j] = 0}
status: fails
counterexample: a = [4, 0, 9], i = 1, j = 1
why:
  The precondition holds: $a[1] = 0$, and position $1$ exists. The write
  sets $a[1]$ to $1$, so $a[j] = 0$ is false afterwards. When $i = j$,
  $a[i]$ and $a[j]$ name the same cell: they are aliases. Substitution on
  the text of the formula cannot see it.

step: R1, the weakest precondition of a[i] := 1
rule: ARRAY-ASSIGN, WP
focus: i1
text:
  The statement has the shape $a[I] := E$ with $I = i$ and $E = 1$.
  ARRAY-ASSIGN substitutes the array $a$ in $Q$ with the updated array
  $a[i |-> 1]$, and adds $def(a[i])$ for the write. $E$ reads no array, so
  nothing else is added.
calc:
  wp(a[i] := 1, Q)
  = (a[j] = 0)[a <- a[i |-> 1]] && def(a[i])
  = a[i |-> 1][j] = 0 && def(a[i])     // the a of Q is replaced by a[i |-> 1]
  = ((j = i && 1 = 0) || (j != i && a[j] = 0)) && def(a[i])     // select-store: j may or may not be i, so we keep both cases
  = ((j = i && false) || (j != i && a[j] = 0)) && def(a[i])     // 1 = 0 is false
  = (false || (j != i && a[j] = 0)) && def(a[i])     // A && false is false
  = j != i && a[j] = 0 && def(a[i])     // false || B is B
  = j != i && a[j] = 0 && 0 <= i < sizeof(a)     // def(a[i]) is 0 <= i < sizeof(a)
set: R1 = j != i && a[j] = 0 && 0 <= i < sizeof(a)

step: P implies R1
rule: CONSEQUENCE
focus: P, R1
text:
  $R1$ is the weakest precondition of the statement for $Q$. By the
  consequence rule, the triple is valid when the precondition $P$ implies
  $R1$.
  >>> «P» ==> «R1»
vc: j != i && a[j] = 0 && 0 <= i < sizeof(a) ==> j != i && a[j] = 0 && 0 <= i < sizeof(a)
why:
  $P$ and $R1$ are the same formula.
result: The triple is valid. Its precondition contains $j != i$, the condition that the naive rule misses.

summary:
  The two preconditions differ only by $j != i$. In the counterexample
  $i = j = 1$ this condition is false, so the correct rule makes no
  promise about that execution. That is right, since the postcondition
  fails there.
